.Dd October 2, 2026
.Dt GETXATTR 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm getxattr ,
.Nm setxattr ,
.Nm listxattr ,
.Nm removexattr
.Nd extended attributes, which no file system here stores
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/xattr.h
.Ft ssize_t
.Fn getxattr "const char *path" "const char *name" "void *value" "size_t size"
.Ft int
.Fn setxattr "const char *path" "const char *name" "const void *value" "size_t size" "int flags"
.Ft ssize_t
.Fn listxattr "const char *path" "char *list" "size_t size"
.Ft int
.Fn removexattr "const char *path" "const char *name"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
An extended attribute is a name and a value kept with a file outside its
data.
No file system on Rach2 stores them, and these calls answer as Linux does
for a file system built without them:
.Fn getxattr ,
.Fn setxattr
and
.Fn removexattr
fail with
.Er EOPNOTSUPP ,
and
.Fn listxattr
returns 0, an empty list.
.Pp
Each call first finds the file, so a path that names nothing fails as it
would for
.Xr stat 2 .
The
.Fn lgetxattr ,
.Fn lsetxattr ,
.Fn llistxattr
and
.Fn lremovexattr
forms act on a final symbolic link itself, and the
.Fn fgetxattr ,
.Fn fsetxattr ,
.Fn flistxattr
and
.Fn fremovexattr
forms on an open descriptor in place of
.Fa path .
.Pp
The i386 system call numbers are 226 to 237: set, get, list and remove,
each as the path, link and descriptor forms in that order.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn listxattr
returns 0.
The others return -1 with
.Va errno
set; the kernel returns the negative error number.
.Sh EXAMPLES
GNU
.Xr cp 1
with
.Fl a ,
.Xr tar 1
with
.Fl -xattrs
and
.Xr setfattr 1
reach these calls; the first two take
.Er EOPNOTSUPP
to mean a file system without attributes and go on.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "[EOPNOTSUPP]"
.It Bq Er EBADF
The descriptor is not open, or was opened with
.Dv O_PATH .
.It Bq Er EFAULT
.Fa path
is not readable.
.It Bq Er ENOENT
.Fa path
names nothing.
.It Bq Er ENOTDIR
A directory in
.Fa path
is not a directory.
.It Bq Er EOPNOTSUPP
The file system stores no extended attributes, which is every one here.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr chattr 1 ,
.Xr stat 2
